  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rights-based labour migration for integration enhanced under Common Market  Protocal</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO&#x2019;s Country Programme Outcome (CPO) focuses on strengthening rights-based labour migration under the East African Community&apos;s Common Market Protocol. The primary aim is to enhance the integration and protection of migrant workers while facilitating their safe and dignified movement across borders. The CPO addresses critical issues such as inadequate legal frameworks, lack of social protection, and the need for robust partnerships to promote decent work opportunities for migrants.

Expected outcomes include the development of regional policy frameworks and national legislation that ensure fair and effective labour migration governance, benefiting migrant workers, especially women and youth. The CPO will also focus on creating competency standards for sectors like domestic work, thus facilitating skill recognition and portability among member states. Through the establishment of dialogue forums among social partners, the initiative aims to foster collaboration between sending and receiving countries, improving conditions for migrant workers in sectors with high demands.

The primary beneficiaries of this initiative are migrant workers from IGAD member states seeking employment abroad, particularly in the Middle East and within the East African region. By working closely with the East African Community and regional stakeholders, ILO will provide technical support to strengthen capacities for implementing fair recruitment practices and developing bilateral labour agreements that prioritize migrant workers&apos; rights.

This collaborative effort is set against a backdrop of rising migration trends in the region, driven by economic opportunities abroad. The ILO&#x2019;s engagement is crucial in ensuring that ethical migration practices are promoted and that migrant workers receive the protections they deserve, ultimately contributing to sustainable economic development in Eastern Africa.</narrative>
  </description>
